WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.263
SAAD ALI: Hello, everyone.

00:00:01.263 --> 00:00:03.750
We're here at KubeCon
Barcelona with Dustin Kirkland.

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:06.840
Dustin is Product
Manager of GKE.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:10.050
Dustin is going to be
telling us about Windows.

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:11.070
Windows in Google.

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:11.720
Why?

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:12.390
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah.

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:14.807
It's something that we've heard
quite a bit from customers

00:00:14.807 --> 00:00:15.840
lately.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.360
Those of us around
the Linux ecosystem

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:21.120
have long been putting our
workloads, our applications

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:24.180
into Linux containers and
using Kubernetes to orchestrate

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:25.560
those containers.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.770
Until recently, that's
really not been available

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:29.850
for Windows workloads.

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:32.020
And half of the
world's workloads

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:35.970
are still developed on
Windows and for Windows.

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:38.940
So with the release of
Windows Server 2019,

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:41.970
the Windows Server kernel has
built into it functionality

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:45.250
that operates very much
like Linux containers.

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:47.970
And so in the upstream
Kubernetes community,

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:50.640
Microsoft, Google, and a
number of other companies

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:53.520
have been instrumental in adding
support for Windows Server

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.460
containers to Kubernetes.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:59.000
And as of Kubernetes 1.14, the
upstream open source release

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.940
of Kubernetes has declared
Windows container support

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:04.516
generally available.

00:01:04.516 --> 00:01:05.349
SAAD ALI: Very cool.

00:01:05.349 --> 00:01:06.930
So what kind of
features are going

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:08.860
to be available in this release?

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:10.902
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah, so
you should really think

00:01:10.902 --> 00:01:13.320
about your basic applications.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:16.830
We're focused on,
largely, first-party apps,

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:19.410
the apps that either you have
the source code to and you

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:22.140
develop yourself
on top of Windows,

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:25.590
or apps that are being
developed, new .NET apps,

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:30.560
new Visual Basic, or C++,
we see a lot of in Windows.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:35.580
So new apps that can be compiled
and built into a container

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:41.820
image we can deploy now directly
to GKE as a Windows application

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:43.500
on a Windows node.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:45.690
And then, importantly, we
get this question a lot,

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:48.330
but yes, you can have
Windows nodes and Linux

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:49.770
nodes in the same cluster.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:51.220
Those pods can communicate.

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:54.270
You can have applications,
a .NET application running

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:58.110
in a Windows Server container
that's talking to a MySQL

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:00.385
service running in
a Linux container.

00:02:00.385 --> 00:02:01.510
SAAD ALI: That's very cool.

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:03.930
So you're going to have
Windows and Linux coexisting

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:05.370
in a single GKE cluster.

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:07.530
When can customers get
their hands on this?

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:09.840
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah,
so as of GKE 114,

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:13.740
the Rapid release channel, we'll
have the functionality built

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:14.880
into it.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:18.360
We're making that available
in an early access program,

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:21.600
and we're onboarding
our first customers now.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:23.570
We have a sign-up
link that we can

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:26.580
send you to to sign up
for that early access.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:29.220
We'll run it through its
paces, first an early access,

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:31.937
then an alpha, then a
beta, and then a GA.

00:02:31.937 --> 00:02:33.270
SAAD ALI: That's super exciting.

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:34.570
Well, we're looking
forward to that.

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:35.460
Thank you very much, Dustin

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:36.460
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Thanks.

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:39.110
[MUSIC PLAYING]

